= Surgery Short Cases & Long Cases

== Short Cases

=== 1. Thyroid / Multinodular Goiter
- *Clinical Signs & Examination:* Inspection and palpation findings. Kocher's sign (how to do, why stridor happens, tracheal cartilage/fascia anatomy). Berry's sign (how to do, interpretation, carotid artery level). Tell 3 features of the hand (resting vs. exercise pulse). Eye signs. Name of the ligament that makes it move with deglutition. Lymph node palpation.
- *Toxicity & Euthyroid:* Why euthyroid? Euthyroid stage pulse. Toxic features (can they present without eye signs?). If toxicity features develop long after gland enlargement, what is it called?
- *Malignancy & Metastasis:* Benign vs. malignant differentiation (consistency, surface). Types of carcinoma (papillary vs. follicular). Which malignancy is better and why? Lymphatic vs. hematogenous/bone metastasis. 
- *Investigations:* USG importance. FNAC vs. Biopsy (capsular invasion in follicular). Thyroid scan (affinity for normal vs. abnormal tissue, why it might not be done here). TSH, T3, T4 levels (external examiners often ask this).
- *Management & Complications:* Indications for surgery. Treatment plan if the patient refuses surgery (counseling). Name of the surgery. Complications: which nerves are injured? (Right/Left recurrent laryngeal nerve injury effects, bilateral injury effects).

=== 2. Inguinal Hernia
- *Examination:* Full procedure. "Get above the swelling" test (findings, interpretation). Deep ring occlusion test (landmarks, how to do it, interpretation of positive/negative results). Visible vs. palpable cough impulse. Differentiating hernia from a pure testicular swelling. What if the patient is uncooperative? 
- *Diagnosis & Classification:* Direct vs. indirect (which is more dangerous and why?). Complete vs. incomplete. Reducible vs. irreducible vs. obstructed vs. strangulated. Content of the sac (e.g., intestine = enterocele, omentum, or bladder). 
- *Anatomy:* Boundaries of Hesselbach's triangle. Boundaries of the superficial ring. Mid-inguinal point vs. mid-point of inguinal ligament (clinical importance).
- *Special Scenarios:* Patient with BPH and Hernia (which to treat first?). Agricultural worker who wants to delay surgery for a month (can it be delayed? When can it *not* be delayed, e.g., in children). 
- *Management:* Herniotomy vs. Herniorrhaphy. Incision used. How to identify the deep ring during surgery. Mesh placement (onlay vs. inlay), what lies over it, how to close.

=== 3. Lipoma / Lipomatosis
- *Examination & Signs:* Inspection. Fluctuation test, indentation test, slipping sign (why do we do them?). Lymph node examination (why do we check?). 
- *Diagnosis & D/D:* Why lipoma? Differentiating from sebaceous cyst and neurofibroma. (Neurofibromatosis family history, differentiating features). If a swelling is in the midline of a child's back over the spine, what is it? (Meningocele, check transillumination).
- *Special Types:* Lipomatosis (multiple lipomas in the neck/back). Painful lipoma condition name (Adiposis dolorosa/Dercum's disease).
- *Management:* Complications of lipoma. Indications for surgery. Malignant transformation (features of malignancy, investigations to confirm). 

=== 4. Ulcer
- *Examination:* Inspection and palpation. Differentiating base and floor (what lies where?). Edge vs. margin. Surrounding skin examination (prominent veins like Great Saphenous vein). Pulse check (Arteria dorsalis pedis—location, origin, congenital absence percentage). 
- *Classification & Causes:* Edge-wise classification with examples (e.g., squamous cell carcinoma, syphilitic, tubercular, punched-out). Venous vs. arterial vs. neuropathic ulcer locations. Is the ulcer natural or man-made (e.g., amputation site)?
- *Healing & Complications:* Pinkish granulation tissue. Causes of non-healing (nutrition, blood supply, infection). Malignant transformation (Marjolin's ulcer—how to diagnose, wedge biopsy technique).
- *Management:* Treatment options. Dressings (regular vs. specific). Grafting (types of grafts, prerequisites for grafting, why graft instead of letting it heal by secondary intention to prevent contractures). Secondary closure.

=== 5. Hypospadias
- *Examination:* Full procedure. Demonstrating chordee. Checking the back/spine. Identifying the meatus position. Checking for murmurs.
- *Diagnosis & Concepts:* Definition of chordee. Other associated genitourinary congenital anomalies. 
- *Management:* Flap options and surgical stages (bringing meatus up). Contraindications (why we *never* circumcise these patients initially). Management if a patient refuses the 2nd stage operation.

=== 6. Breast Lump / Breast Carcinoma
- *History & Presentation:* Presenting the case (must include breastfeeding, OCP, HRT history). Early breast carcinoma presentation. Site and size descriptions (e.g., lower inner, 4x3). Nipple discharge types (blood vs. serous vs. serosanguinous, pathological vs. physiological/milky). 
- *Investigations & Staging:* Triple assessment (USG vs. Mammography indications). Core biopsy vs. FNAC (which is better and why). MRI advantages. Staging investigations. Gene names and screening protocols (SBE timing, family history guidelines).
- *Management:* MRM (Modified Radical Mastectomy) definition. Neo-adjuvant therapy advantages. 

