== Long Cases

=== 1. Chronic Cholecystitis & Choledocholithiasis
- *Presentation & Signs:* Salient features. Murphy's sign demonstration. Duodenal point and gastric point tenderness. Pain radiation vs. referred pain (tip of shoulder). Charcot's triad (for Choledocholithiasis). Intermittent vs. progressive vs. fluctuating jaundice. 
- *D/D:* How to differentiate clinically from PUD, Chronic Pancreatitis, or Gastric Ca. Changes in bowel habits. 
- *Investigations:* USG findings (gallbladder size, posterior acoustic shadow vs. fixed polyp shadow). When to do Upper GI Endoscopy (biopsy sites, identifying poorly differentiated Ca). ERCP/Cholangiogram.
- *Management:* Medical management (Ochsner-Sherren regimen). Open vs. Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy (advantages, ports, gas used, complications). Structures injured during Lap Chole (Calot's triangle boundaries, right hepatic artery). 
- *Surgical Details:* Incisions (midline vs. right subcostal/paramedian). Layers cut in a midline incision, advantages/disadvantages, incisional hernia check (head rising test, cough impulse). Choledocholithotomy (T-tube management, why use Vitamin K, when to remove tube).

=== 2. Chronic Pancreatitis
- *Presentation & Signs:* Characteristics of pain (compared to PUD). Pain relief position (leaning forward). Steatorrhea. Personal history (alcohol, smoking). Ascites mechanism and shifting dullness demonstration. 
- *Concepts:* FCPD (Fibrocalculous Pancreatic Diabetes) with DM. Why is it termed "acute on chronic" pancreatitis rather than just chronic? 
- *Investigations:* USG and X-ray findings. Confirmatory tests.
- *Management:* Medical vs. surgical management. Stone in head/body/tail (specific operations). Indications for surgery. Treatment for hematemesis.

=== 3. Gastric Outlet Obstruction (GOO) due to Pyloric Stenosis
- *Examination:* Auscultopercussion test, Succussion splash (purposes, how long it can be elicited and why). 
- *Metabolic Changes:* Paradoxical aciduria (mechanism). Electrolyte imbalances and correction plans.
- *Management & Complications:* Stomach preparation (indications, fluid used, volume). Surgical options (gastrojejunostomy terms: posterior, retrocolic, iso-peristaltic, short loop). Complications of surgery (Dumping syndrome types, vascular dumping).

=== 4. Ca Rectum / GI Malignancies
- *Presentation:* Rectal bleeding in adults vs. children. Cough/hemoptysis history (lung metastasis). 
- *Investigations:* Colonoscopy vs. Sigmoidoscopy vs. Proctoscopy (lengths, 160cm limit). Tumor markers. Staging investigations. 
- *Metastasis:* Lymphatic vs. blood-borne (routes, liver palpation, tender hepatomegaly). Krukenberg tumor (mechanism, who gets it). 

